---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import { SITE } from '../lib/site';

const title = 'Privacy — Your Files Never Leave Your Browser | NoWatermark';
const description =
  'How NoWatermark handles your data: files are processed locally and never uploaded, there is no account, no storage and no file-derived analytics.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonicalPath="/privacy">
  <div class="nw-shell-narrow pt-8">
    <Breadcrumbs trail={[{ href: '/privacy', label: 'Privacy' }]} />

    <header class="mt-6">
      <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold">Privacy</h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-lg nw-muted">
        The short version: your files never leave your browser, and we could not read them if we
        wanted to.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="nw-content mt-10">
      <h2>Files you scan or clean</h2>
      <p>
        Images and text you put into our tools are processed entirely on your own device. There is
        no upload endpoint on this site. There is no server that receives files, no database, and no
        object storage. We do not have a copy of your file, and we never did.
      </p>
      <p>Specifically, we never transmit or store:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>your file's contents, in whole or in part</li>
        <li>hashes or fingerprints derived from your file</li>
        <li>your filenames</li>
        <li>metadata values extracted from your file, including GPS, timestamps and author fields</li>
        <li>text you paste into the text tools</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        You can confirm this yourself: load a tool page, disconnect from the internet, and use it.
        Everything works. Or watch your browser's network panel while you scan — nothing carrying
        your file appears.
      </p>

      <h2>Accounts</h2>
      <p>
        There are none. Every tool works without registration, and we hold no user records because
        there are no users to record.
      </p>

      <h2>Analytics</h2>
      <p>
        We use Cloudflare Web Analytics to understand which pages people find useful. It is
        privacy-focused: it does not use cookies, does not fingerprint visitors, and does not track
        people across sites. It records page URLs, referrers, general performance timings and coarse
        location at country level.
      </p>
      <p>
        No analytics event carries anything derived from your file or your pasted text. Analytics is
        also entirely optional to the product: if you block it, every tool continues to work
        normally.
      </p>

      <h2>Cookies</h2>
      <p>
        We do not set cookies for analytics or tracking. If we introduce advertising in future, ad
        providers may set their own cookies, and this page will be updated to say so plainly before
        that happens.
      </p>

      <h2>Advertising</h2>
      <p>
        This site is not currently running advertising. If it does in future, ad slots will sit in
        clearly separated areas, never adjacent to download controls or styled to resemble product
        buttons — and advertising will never require your file to be uploaded, because uploading is
        not a thing this site does.
      </p>

      <h2>Hosting</h2>
      <p>
        The site is served as static files by Cloudflare Pages. Cloudflare processes standard
        request information such as IP address and user agent in order to deliver the page and
        protect against abuse, as any web host does. That is request-level data about visiting a
        page, unrelated to the files you process.
      </p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>
        If this policy changes materially — particularly anything touching the local-processing
        guarantee — we will say so on this page rather than quietly editing it.
      </p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Questions about privacy can go to <a href={`mailto:${SITE.contactEmail}`}>{SITE.contactEmail}</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="h-16"></div>
</BaseLayout>
